        "current_path": "philosophy/06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:philosophy-of-science",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[philosophy, of]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "06 - Philosophy of Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "PROBLEMS:\n  Universal statements (\"All copper conducts electricity\") are not directly verifiable\n    (can only observe finite instances; never exhaustively test)\n  The principle of verifiability is itself neither analytic nor empirically verifiable →\n    self-defeating by its own criterion\n  Theoretical terms: electrons, quarks, fields — not directly observable; how to verify?\n```"
    },
