        "root_concepts": "[philosophy, of]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "06 - Philosophy of Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nTHOMAS KUHN (The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, 1962):\n  Most influential philosophy of science work of 20th century; changed how science is discussed"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md#023",
      "path": [
        "06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:06-philosophy-of-science, git-history:philosophy:06-philosophy-of-science]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md",
        "concepts": "[philosophy, of, science]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:philosophy-of-science",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[philosophy, of]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "06 - Philosophy of Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "PARADIGM: an exemplary achievement that defines the field; framework of assumptions,\n  methods, problems, and standards for a scientific community\n  Normal science: puzzle-solving within the paradigm; doesn't question fundamental assumptions\n  Not just a theory: includes instruments, exemplars, values, metaphysical commitments"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md#024",
      "path": [
        "06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:06-philosophy-of-science, git-history:philosophy:06-philosophy-of-science]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md",
        "concepts": "[philosophy, of, science]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:philosophy-of-science",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[philosophy, of]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "06 - Philosophy of Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "STAGES OF SCIENTIFIC DEVELOPMENT:\n  Pre-paradigm science: multiple competing schools; no consensus framework\n  Normal science: Big paradigm in place; fill in the details; refine the theory\n    Puzzles: problems you try to solve within the paradigm\n    Anomalies: observations that don't fit; normal science tries to explain them away\n  Crisis: accumulation of anomalies that paradigm cannot handle; normal science fails\n    Extraordinary science: fundamental assumptions questioned; competing alternatives appear\n  Revolution: paradigm shift to new paradigm; old paradigm abandoned"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md#025",
      "path": [
        "06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:06-philosophy-of-science, git-history:philosophy:06-philosophy-of-science]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md",
        "concepts": "[philosophy, of, science]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:philosophy-of-science",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[philosophy, of]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "06 - Philosophy of Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "INCOMMENSURABILITY:\n  Old and new paradigms cannot be directly compared in a common neutral vocabulary\n  Different problems, different standards, different meanings of key terms\n  Newton's \"mass\" and Einstein's \"mass\" are not the same concept\n  Rational reconstruction post-hoc: we tell stories about why new paradigm is \"better,\"\n    but this is not the logic that drove the actual change\n  Kuhn: paradigm choice is partly non-rational (gestalt switch; social factors)"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md#026",
      "path": [
        "06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:06-philosophy-of-science, git-history:philosophy:06-philosophy-of-science]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md",
        "concepts": "[philosophy, of, science]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:philosophy-of-science",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[philosophy, of]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "06 - Philosophy of Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "EXAMPLES:\n  Ptolemaic → Copernican → Newtonian → Einsteinian astronomy\n  Phlogiston → Lavoisier's oxygen-based combustion\n  Newtonian → quantum/relativistic mechanics\n  Plate tectonics revolution (1960s): previously \"obvious\" constraints (continents don't move)\n    overturned; geologists' entire research program reorganized"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md#027",
      "path": [
        "06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:philosophy:06-philosophy-of-science, git-history:philosophy:06-philosophy-of-science]",
        "canonical_path": "philosophy/06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md",
        "concepts": "[philosophy, of, science]",
        "current_path": "philosophy/06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md",
        "id": "maxim:philosophy:philosophy-of-science",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "philosophy",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[philosophy, of]",
        "section": "philosophy",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "06 - Philosophy of Science",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "CRITICISMS:\n  Irrationalism: if paradigm choice is not fully rational, science is just \"mob psychology\"\n    Kuhn's response: there are values that persist across paradigm changes (accuracy, consistency,\n      breadth, simplicity, fruitfulness); just insufficient to uniquely determine choice\n  What is a paradigm?: Kuhn identified 21 different senses; concept vague\n  Progress: Kuhn later acknowledged scientific progress exists, even across paradigms\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md#028",
      "path": [
        "06-PHILOSOPHY-OF-SCIENCE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
